    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two problems jeopardize the meaningfulness of geological curvature analysis: one is the manner in which curvature is calculated, and the other is the way in which calculated curvatures are applied to solve geological problems. In curvature calculations, problems arise due to the sampled nature of geological horizon data. Calculated curvatures of sampled geological surfaces are inherently flawed (band limited) according to the Shannon sampling theorem and thus problematic if calculated without prior data smoothing. In cases where the surface is over-sampled with respect to the scale of investigation, the data should be smoothed and calculated curvatures will become meaningful. A problem associated with curvature analysis is that the second partial derivative of the surface is often used as a measure for curvature. Not only does the second partial derivative face the same sample problems as curvature calculation when calculated over a grid, it also happens to be an erroneous proxy for curvature. In terms of the usefulness of curvature analysis to help solve problems in structural geology, Gaussian curvature is a valid tool for surface description, but is a very poor tool when applied to detect zones of elevated deformation or fracture density. Calculated curvature is not a good indicator for fracture density, however, it can successfully be used in thin plate theory to calculate pure bending strains. The application of thin plate theory, however, is limited to pure bending and infinitesimal strains. Therefore, when dealing with geological fold and dome structures that include stretching of strata and finite strains, a finite-element analysis is suggested.",
